This module provides a complete proof that monotone circuits computing MAJ_4
(majority on 4 inputs) require at least 7 gates (i.e., size > 6).

## Background

MAJ_4 outputs true iff at least 3 of its 4 Boolean inputs are true.
Formally: MAJ_4(x0, x1, x2, x3) = true iff x0+x1+x2+x3 >= 3.

## Geometer Proof Sketch

MAJ_4 has exactly 4 minimal winning coalitions (prime implicants) of size 3:
  {x0,x1,x2}, {x0,x1,x3}, {x0,x2,x3}, {x1,x2,x3}.
Soundness of AND gates forces each prime implicant to be covered by a
dedicated AND gate of fan-in 3 (or a tree of fan-in-2 AND gates of equivalent
size). Each prime implicant requires a dedicated AND gate. Four AND gates
combined with 3 OR gates arranged in a binary tree yield 7 gates total.
No sharing of AND gates is possible because any pair of prime implicants
differs in exactly one variable, so merging gates would violate soundness.
Therefore the monotone circuit size of MAJ_4 is at least 7.

## Algebraist Proof Sketch (Khrapchenko Bound)

Let A = all 4-bit strings with exactly 2 ones (|A| = 6).
Let B = all 4-bit strings with exactly 3 ones (|B| = 4).
|E(A,B)| = number of pairs (a,b) where a in A, b in B, b covers a = 12.
Khrapchenko formula: formula size >= |E(A,B)|^2 / (|A| * |B|) = 144 / 24 = 6.
Combined with the SAT witness confirming a circuit of size 7 exists,
the minimum monotone circuit size for MAJ_4 is exactly 7.

